yeetdrop
========

AirDrop, on Linux. Your iPhone sends a photo, your laptop gets a photo.
Apple did not ask for this.

    curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/teddytennant/yeetdrop/main/install.sh | sh

That grabs the binary from GitHub Releases, then runs `yeetdrop setup`,
which installs packages, builds OWL, and puts everything where it goes,
with progress bars, like a civilised program.

Use it
------

    yeetdrop on              become discoverable, receive files. Ctrl-C to quit
    yeetdrop on -t 2h        stay up for 2 hours (also: 90s, forever)
    yeetdrop send cat.jpg    send to whatever is nearby
    yeetdrop send --to AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF a.pdf b.pdf
    yeetdrop peers           what can we hear
    yeetdrop status          are we visible
    yeetdrop off             close the window from another terminal
    yeetdrop doctor          why is nothing working

On the phone: AirDrop -> Everyone for 10 Minutes. Yes, Apple made you do
that. Received files land in ~/Downloads.

Need
----

 - A Linux box with a MediaTek MT7925 (Framework 13 and friends). It is
   the only card anyone has tested. Others: `yeetdrop setup --allow-untested`
   and tell us how it went.
 - Your access point on channel 6, 44 or 149. AWDL shares the channel so
   Wi-Fi stays connected. `yeetdrop doctor` will tell you if you're not.
 - Bluetooth on (it pokes the phone awake).
 - apt, pacman or dnf. Setup handles the rest.

How
---

Apple's AWDL runs in userspace through OWL on a monitor interface next to
your normal Wi-Fi. The AirDrop protocol (/Discover, /Ask, /Upload over TLS)
comes from the omdrop projects. yeetdrop is the part that wasn't there:
one Rust binary that sets it all up, runs the radio with a single teardown
path instead of a bash script, and doesn't make you read a PKGBUILD.

setup fetches pinned commits (nothing vendored), applies two small patches
to OWL, and installs root-owned into /usr/lib/yeetdrop. Nothing runs until
you say `on`.

Known problems
--------------

 - Deleting a monitor interface can wedge the MT7925's receive path, so
   teardown forces a reconnect. Wi-Fi blips for a second when you stop.
 - Sending is slower than receiving (~0.5 MB/s vs ~1.3 MB/s).
 - iPhones randomise their AWDL address. Devices show as MACs, not names.
 - The Rust supervisor is new. The stack under it is not, but expect bugs.

Build it yourself
-----------------

    cargo build --release
    ./target/release/yeetdrop setup

Credit
------

This exists because of https://github.com/t4t5/omdrop-owl, which got
AirDrop working on the MT7925 and wrote the hard parts down. The radio
approach, the OWL patches, the hardware notes and the MT7925 teardown fix
are all theirs. yeetdrop is that idea with a nicer front door.

Standing on:

 - OWL (seemoo-lab), via the jedbillyb/owl fork   github.com/jedbillyb/owl
 - omdrop-awdl, sender and BLE wake               github.com/brentkearney/omdrop-awdl
 - omdrop-plugin, the receiver                    github.com/brentkearney/omdrop-plugin
 - OpenDrop (seemoo-lab), which reverse engineered AirDrop first.
   Different project, same itch. Go look at theirs too.
                                                  github.com/seemoo-lab/opendrop

